    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Screen-and-treat strategy summary recommendations",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-screen-and-treat-strategy-summary-recommendation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1\t\nThe expert panel includes all members of the WHO Steering Group, the Guideline Development Group (GDG), and the \nExternal Review Group (ERG).\nThe expert panel1 recommends against the use of CKC as a treatment in a screen-\nand-treat strategy. Therefore, all screen-and-treat strategies below involve treatment with \ncryotherapy, or LEEP when the patient is not eligible for cryotherapy.\nThe expert panel suggests: \nf\nf Use a strategy of screen with an HPV test and treat, over a strategy of screen with VIA and \ntreat. In resource-constrained settings, where screening with an HPV test is not feasi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "Summarises the screen-and-treat recommendations, beginning with advice against cold knife conization so that all strategies use cryotherapy, or LEEP when cryotherapy is unsuitable. It sets out the panel's suggested preferences between strategies based on HPV testing, VIA and cytology with colposcopy, including options for resource-limited settings and countries with existing quality cytology programmes. The section also begins the background, describing how CIN is graded by the thickness of abnormal epithelium.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) is a \npremalignant lesion that may exist at any one \nof three stages: CIN1, CIN2, or CIN3.2 If left \nuntreated, CIN2 or CIN3 (collectively referred \nto as CIN2+) can progress to cervical cancer. \nIt is estimated that approximately 1–2% of \nwomen have CIN2+ each year. This rate is \nreported to be higher in women of HIV-positive \nstatus, at 10% (1–5). The standard practice is \nto screen women using cytology (Pap test), \nand when cytology results are positive the \ndiagnosis of CIN is based on subsequent \ncolposcopy, biopsy of suspicious lesions, \nand then",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "Introduces the goal of a screen-and-treat programme: reducing cervical cancer and deaths with few adverse events, linked to treatment and referral for invasive cancer. It explains how single tests and sequences of tests are used and how women with discordant results are managed. It notes that the recommendations rest on evidence about test accuracy and the benefits and harms of treatment.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Target audience",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-target-audience.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guideline is intended primarily for \npolicy-makers, managers, programme officers, \nand other professionals in the health sector \nwho have responsibility for choosing strategies \nfor cervical cancer prevention, at country, \nregional and district levels. Individuals working \nin reproductive health care programmes, \nparticularly programmes for the prevention of \nsexually transmitted infections (STIs) including \nHIV/AIDS and for family planning, at the district \nand primary health care levels, should also \nconsult these guidelines to understand how \nrecommendations are developed and why it \ni",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Identifies the guideline's main audience as health policy-makers, managers and programme officers who choose cervical cancer prevention strategies, as well as reproductive health, STI, HIV and family planning staff.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Purpose",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-purpose.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guideline provides recommendations \nfor screen-and-treat strategies to prevent \ncervical cancer (Chapter 3). In addition to the \nrecommendations, this document proposes \na decision-making flowchart for choosing the \nbest screen-and-treat strategy for a particular \nsetting at a programme level (Annex 2); and \n– for use once the strategy is chosen – the \nscreen-and-treat flowcharts for all strategies \nare provided, including the flowcharts \nspecifically for women of negative or unknown \nHIV status (Annex 3), and those for women \nof HIV-positive status or of unknown HIV \nstatus in areas with",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out what the guideline contains: screen-and-treat recommendations, a decision flowchart for choosing a strategy, and flowcharts for each strategy by HIV status. It also describes the methods used and points to online GRADE evidence profiles and evidence-to-recommendation tables.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
