    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guideline groups",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-guideline-groups.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO formed a Guideline Development Group \n(GDG) for the screen-and-treat strategies to \nprevent cervical cancer, chaired by Joanna \nCain. The 17 selected members provided expert \nclinical guidance and support throughout \nthe guideline development process. WHO \nalso selected an External Review Group \n(ERG) comprising 33 professionals, including \nhealth-care providers with experience in \nscreening and treating CIN, pathologists, \nresearchers in cervical cancer prevention \nand treatment, programme directors, health \neducators, epidemiologists, public health \nofficers, nurses and methodologists. A",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the three groups formed to develop the guideline: a 17-member Guideline Development Group, a 33-member External Review Group and a Methods Group from McMaster University.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Formulating questions and determining outcomes",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-formulating-questions-and-determining-outcomes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "outcomes\nIn February 2011, the GDG met to discuss \nthe questions and outcomes to address in \nthe chapter on screen-and-treat strategies \nto appear in the updated C4-GEP, in order to \nincorporate new evidence. The GDG identified \n15 potential questions to guide the evidence \nreview for screening options and treatment \nstrategies for cervical pre-cancer. The MG \nsurveyed the GDG anonymously online using \nSurvey Monkey5 to prioritize the questions and \ndetermine which ones are clinically relevant \nor used in practice; 14 out of 17 members \nresponded. Among the 15 questions, the \nGDG identified 7",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how the Guideline Development Group in 2011 narrowed 15 possible questions to eight through an online survey, dropping comparisons not used in practice. It then describes how outcomes such as cancer, mortality and treatment complications were ranked, with those rated important or higher included in the evidence review.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Synthesis of the evidence and preparation of evidence profiles",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-synthesis-of-the-evidence-and-preparation-of-evi.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "preparation of evidence profiles\nA screen-and-treat strategy is made up of two \nlinked parts: the screening test(s) followed by \ntreatment of CIN. The best studies to inform \nscreen-and-treat recommendations are \nrandomized controlled trials in which women \nare randomized to receive ‘screen-and-treat \nstrategy A’ or ‘screen-and-treat strategy B’, \nand health outcomes of all of the women are \nmeasured and presented (even those who \nhad false-negative screening results and were \nnever treated). However, few such studies have \nbeen conducted; most studies do not link the \nscreening strategy with",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that few randomized trials link screening strategies to treatment and patient outcomes, so the evidence came mostly from separate studies of test accuracy and of outcomes after treatment. It describes the systematic reviews, pooling of data and use of GRADE to rate evidence quality. It adds that evidence-to-recommendation tables were prepared summarising benefits, harms, values, resource use and feasibility.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Modelling of health outcomes",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-modelling-of-health-outcomes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A screening test with the highest diagnostic \naccuracy is not necessarily the test of choice \nin clinical practice. The decision to recommend \na screening test needs to be justified by its \nimpact on downstream patient-important health \noutcomes. Decision analysis is a powerful tool \nfor evaluating a diagnostic or screening test \non the basis of long-term patient-important \noutcomes when only intermediate outcomes \n– such as test sensitivity and specificity – \nare known. When making the decision to \nrecommend a diagnostic or screening test, a \npanel should consider the health outcomes \ndownstr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the most accurate test is not necessarily the best choice, so a decision-analysis model was built to estimate downstream health outcomes of true and false screening results. The model calculated these for VIA, HPV testing and cytology, with assumptions set in advance and sensitivity analyses by HIV status and age.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Development of the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 26,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-development-of-the-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In early 2012 (26–28 April), the GDG, \nthe ERG and the MG met to discuss the \nrecommendations. One member each from \nthe GDG and the MG chaired the meeting, \nwhich was attended by experts from around \nthe world, representing various public health \nand medical disciplines. To expand the \ngeographical representativeness of the GDG, \nit was decided that the ERG – a large group \nwith members representing many countries \n– would participate in the development of \nthe recommendations during that meeting. \nMembers of the MG presented evidence \nprofiles and evidence-to-recommendation \ntables, which in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the April 2012 meeting at which the guideline groups reviewed evidence on benefits, harms, values, resources and feasibility to agree the recommendations. It notes qualitative evidence that women may fear screening and find a second visit burdensome. It explains how strong and conditional recommendations were distinguished, noting that many in this guideline are conditional.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guideline review and approval process",
      "pageStart": 26,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-guideline-review-and-approval-process.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The WHO screen-and-treat strategies to prevent \ncervical cancer underwent the following peer \nreview process before and during development:\n The questions formulated for the development \nof the guidelines were circulated among the \nWHO Steering Group, who also discussed \nthem with the GDG. When the GDG and the \nWHO Steering Group had reached agreement \non the questions, these were sent to the ERG.\n The protocol for systematic reviews was \ncirculated among the GDG. This protocol was \nalso discussed during the ERG meeting, which \nwas also attended by the European Guidelines \nDevelopment Group",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the review process, in which questions, review protocols, evidence and draft recommendations were circulated among the steering, development and external review groups before WHO clearance. It ends with a table explaining what strong and conditional recommendations mean for patients, clinicians and policy-makers.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-94830/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Recommendations",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-3-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
